const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Editing and Translation","serviceType":"Service hub","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/","description":"Proofreading $0.025 to translation $0.12 a word, every rate inside a published band. ISO 17100, ISO 18587 and the reporting guideline your design requires.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Which of your seven services do I need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Use the router at the top of this page. The short version:\n\nIs the writing wrong, or the argument? Writing means copyediting or line editing; argument means scientific editing. Already right and just needs a last check? Proofreading — but only if somebody has edited it before. Has to fit a named journal? Manuscript editing. Has to exist in another language? Translation with editing. A machine has been through it? Post-editing, under a different standard.\n\nIs it a thesis? Thesis editing, whatever the answer to the others, because what an editor may do to a thesis is bounded. Is it a book? Book editing, where most of what matters happens before the edit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between proofreading, copyediting and substantive editing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The difference is the stage of the work and how deep the editor is allowed to go.\n\nProofreaders “check for typographical errors and formatting mistakes” by comparing “the latest stage of the project to the previous one” — $0.025 a word. Copyediting “involves correcting spelling, grammar, usage, and punctuation, checking cross-references, and preparing the style sheets” — $0.035. Line editors work “at the sentence or paragraph level… improving the language and style” — $0.050. Developmental editors deal “with content, organization, and genre considerations” — $0.075 to $0.100.\n\nOn our sample we marked the same 400-word passage as a proofread and a copyedit: fourteen marks against thirty-nine, $62 apart across a 6,200-word manuscript."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you guarantee my paper will be published?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Acceptance is an editor’s judgement about your research — its novelty, its design, its fit with the journal — and no editing or translation service touches any of those.\n\nWhat these services do is narrower and checkable: make the manuscript report what the current reporting guideline asks; bring it inside the word limit without losing content; keep the English out of the way; format it to the journal’s own instructions; and surface the questions — a missing registered outcome, two figures that disagree — while you can still answer them.\n\nThat is worth paying for. It is not the same as improving a paper’s chances."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which standards do you work to?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Different ones for different services, named on each page with the version and the date.\n\nTranslation: ISO 17100:2015 with Amendment 1 of 5 September 2017. Post-editing: ISO 18587:2017, under revision, with ISO/DIS 18587 balloting since 4 September 2026. Evaluation of translated output: ISO 5060:2024. Thesis editing: the IPEd guidelines, July 2025. Manuscripts: the reporting guideline your design requires — CONSORT 2025, PRISMA 2020, STROBE, ARRIVE 2.0, TRIPOD+AI and the rest of the 707 in the EQUATOR library — plus your journal’s own instructions.\n\nWhat we do not display as credentials: COPE, the ICMJE, PRISMA and MARS. Those govern your manuscript, not an editing supplier."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you handle documents a machine or AI tool has already translated?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As a different service under a different standard, and we ask before quoting because the answer changes everything.\n\nMachine output fails fluently: confident, grammatical English that inverts a negation, drops a hedge, or omits a clause without leaving a gap. A language model adds a failure of its own — it improves on the author, which in a research paper is a data-integrity problem.\n\nThe governing standard is ISO 18587:2017, not ISO 17100, which excludes machine translation plus post-editing from its scope. Full post-editing is $0.06 a word, light $0.035. Where the raw output is poor and the destination is publication, we say so after reading a sample rather than after invoicing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I find reliable academic editing and translation services?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The honest answer is a checklist rather than a name, and it works on us as well as on anyone.\n\nDoes the supplier define the service before pricing it? Does the advertised price exist at the cheapest package? Does every tier carry a price at all — or are the cheap ones quoted on request? Does the ladder climb, so each tier contains the one below it and adds a named step? Where a tier is named for how premium it sounds rather than for what it does, that usually suggests something is being skipped. Does it name the standard that governs the work, with a version and a date, rather than badges from bodies that govern something else?\n\nDoes it guarantee perfection? “Flawless” and “100% quality” should end a conversation."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Editing and translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/"}]}];

